@Rindbee I'm not sure, but that may be unrelated. I think it's more that `RemoteTransform2D` just happens to work with its main use case but its logic isn't designed that way.

When you want a `CanvasItem` elsewhere in the scene tree to move along with another `CanvasItem`, you add a `RemoteTransform2D` node as a child and set a target. When you update the parent's `global_position`, the `RemoteTransform2D`'s `global_position` just *happens* to also update, and will match (unless it's offset). However, when you have it set to local, updating the parent's position does nothing for the `RemoteTransform2D`, because its `position` is still whatever it is relative to the parent (99% of the time `0,0`), when the behavior you're looking for is to send the `position` of its parent; the thing you're trying to have the target follow.

Even before your PR, the target will get the local position changes to the `RemoteTransform2D`, it's just it'd never actually change unless you were directly altering the `RemoteTransform2D`'s `position` from a script, which is the exact situation the node was made to avoid. You could just alter the target's `position` directly.

